[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Unit and Integration Testing with Pytest in Flask"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Testing is a critical part of modern web development. It ensures your application behaves as expected, prevents regressions, and gives you confidence when refactoring. In this tutorial, you will learn how to write both unit and integration tests for a Flask application using the powerful pytest framework."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Prerequisites and Setup"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before we begin, ensure you have Python installed. We need to install Flask, pytest, and coverage tools. Run the following command in your terminal to install the necessary packages:"},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"pip install Flask pytest pytest-cov"},{"order":6,"type":"heading","text":"Creating the Flask Application"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"We will use the application factory pattern, which is the recommended way to structure Flask applications for testing. Create a file named app.py with the following code:"},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"from flask import Flask, jsonify, request

def create_app(config_name=None):
    app = Flask(__name__)

    @app.route('/')
    def index():
        return jsonify({"message": "Hello, World!"})

    @app.route('/add', methods=['POST'])
    def add():
        data = request.get_json()
        if not data or 'a' not in data or 'b' not in data:
            return jsonify({"error": "Invalid input"}), 400
        result = data['a'] + data['b']
        return jsonify({"result": result})

    return app

if __name__ == '__main__':
    app = create_app()
    app.run(debug=True)"},{"order":9,"type":"heading","text":"Setting Up the Test Fixtures"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Pytest uses fixtures to manage test environments. We will create a conftest.py file to configure our Flask application instance for testing and set up the test client."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"import pytest
from app import create_app

@pytest.fixture
pyapp():
    app = create_app({"TESTING": True})
    with app.app_context():
        yield app

@pytest.fixture
def client(app):
    return app.test_client()"},{"order":12,"type":"heading","text":"Writing Integration Tests"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Integration tests verify that different parts of your application work together. In Flask, this usually means testing the HTTP routes, request parsing, and JSON responses using the test client. Create a file named test_app.py:"},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"def test_index_route(client):
    response = client.get('/')
    assert response.status_code == 200
    assert response.get_json() == {"message": "Hello, World!"}

def test_add_route_success(client):
    response = client.post('/add', json={"a": 5, "b": 10})
    assert response.status_code == 200
    assert response.get_json() == {"result": 15}

def test_add_route_failure(client):
    response = client.post('/add', json={"a": 5})
    assert response.status_code == 400
    assert response.get_json() == {"error": "Invalid input"}"},{"order":15,"type":"heading","text":"Running the Tests"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"To execute your tests, simply run pytest in your terminal. Pytest will automatically discover files starting with test_ or ending with _test.py."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"pytest -v"},{"order":18,"type":"heading","text":"Best Practices for Flask Testing"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always use a separate configuration or database for testing so you do not corrupt production data."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leverage pytest fixtures for reusable components like database sessions, authentication tokens, and test clients."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Aim for high code coverage by testing both successful code paths and error handling paths."}]
